WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
And we'll get this webinar rolling.

00:07.000 --> 00:09.000
Welcome everybody here.

00:09.000 --> 00:10.000
My name is Cody Winchester.

00:10.000 --> 00:13.000
I am the director of technology at IRE.

00:13.000 --> 00:18.000
Thank you for coming to hear about our brand new resource center.

00:18.000 --> 00:23.000
I'm going to be handing this over to Ben Welch here in a minute.

00:23.000 --> 00:29.000
He and, or the colleague, Derek Willis have been working on,

00:29.000 --> 00:37.000
if you have not seen it, I'm going to drop a link in chat, archive.ire.org,

00:37.000 --> 00:43.000
which is our new repository for contest entries, tip sheets,

00:43.000 --> 00:45.000
all sorts of resources.

00:45.000 --> 00:49.000
We are working on supplementing that as we go.

00:49.000 --> 00:54.000
And Ben, you just recently described this as our 1.0 version.

00:54.000 --> 00:55.000
We're looking for feedback.

00:55.000 --> 00:59.000
So please kick the tires and let us know what you think.

00:59.000 --> 01:03.000
And I know this recording will be made available afterward

01:03.000 --> 01:08.000
for anybody who has to leave early or wasn't able to make it.

01:08.000 --> 01:11.000
And otherwise, I'm going to turn it over to Derek,

01:11.000 --> 01:15.000
chair of the board's website improvement task force,

01:15.000 --> 01:20.000
who's done quite a bit of work to make these resources more accessible.

01:20.000 --> 01:21.000
Thanks.

01:21.000 --> 01:22.000
Thanks, Cody.

01:22.000 --> 01:24.000
And thanks, everybody, for being here.

01:24.000 --> 01:27.000
Really, what I want to, the way I want to begin is by talking about,

01:27.000 --> 01:33.000
like, we, I think, all know or like have an idea of like how valuable

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
the resources that come out of our IRE's trainings and conferences

01:37.000 --> 01:38.000
and contests are.

01:38.000 --> 01:41.000
And like, if you've been a member or been around the organization

01:41.000 --> 01:46.000
or been to one of the conferences, you know that like you go,

01:46.000 --> 01:49.000
sometimes a lot of these sessions you go to for the tip sheets.

01:49.000 --> 01:52.000
The tip sheets are great or the contest entries are great.

01:52.000 --> 01:54.000
You're like, wow, I really want to know how they did that.

01:54.000 --> 01:59.000
And so the effort that our task force and in particular Ben worked on,

01:59.000 --> 02:01.000
has been working on for the last year,

02:01.000 --> 02:06.000
is about trying to harvest that knowledge and that context

02:06.000 --> 02:09.000
and make it accessible to the IRE membership.

02:09.000 --> 02:13.000
And so to that end, like, thanks to the efforts of Cody and other folks

02:13.000 --> 02:17.000
in terms of getting us, you know,

02:17.000 --> 02:22.000
digital representations of conference tip sheets, of contest entries,

02:22.000 --> 02:29.000
we're able to now have a archive site for those and that allows user

02:29.000 --> 02:34.000
and IRE members to search for interesting things, for useful things,

02:34.000 --> 02:36.000
for like, oh, hey, I remember this session from this conference

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
and I want to find that tip sheet or I want to see, you know,

02:39.000 --> 02:43.000
what's been done on this topic and all the, you know,

02:43.000 --> 02:47.000
all the contest entries that we have and, you know, other kinds of materials

02:47.000 --> 02:50.000
from our conferences, IRE's conferences and trainings.

02:50.000 --> 02:55.000
And so that's kind of what we've built with the archive site.

02:55.000 --> 02:58.000
And so we want to kind of show that off and talk a little bit about

02:58.000 --> 03:00.000
what that entails.

03:00.000 --> 03:04.000
Ben, can you do me a favor and just drop in like the presentation link,

03:04.000 --> 03:06.000
because I think I've misplaced it somehow.

03:06.000 --> 03:09.000
I sure can.

03:09.000 --> 03:12.000
And I am clear of the thing I had to watch out for.

03:12.000 --> 03:17.000
Oh, well, then if you'd like to, you give a much better spiel on this

03:17.000 --> 03:21.000
than I do, but it's up to you. I'm happy to.

03:21.000 --> 03:26.000
Let's, I'll pull it up and then let's do edit like Kenny and Dolly.

03:26.000 --> 03:28.000
That sounds great.

03:28.000 --> 03:32.000
All right. Can you guys see my screen?

03:32.000 --> 03:34.000
Yes. Excellent.

03:34.000 --> 03:36.000
So, yeah, you heard it from Derek. There's a brand new website.

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
We launched it at the night car conference in Indianapolis.

03:39.000 --> 03:43.000
You can go to it now if you'd like at archive.iree.org.

03:43.000 --> 03:45.000
It's our new resource center.

03:45.000 --> 03:47.000
And I'm going to tell you how it works.

03:47.000 --> 03:51.000
You can see here on the homepage, which is what you'll see after you log in.

03:51.000 --> 03:55.000
When you first go, you should be prompted to sign in because this is members only.

03:55.000 --> 03:57.000
After you log in, you get a big search box.

03:57.000 --> 04:02.000
And if you type in your own name or the subject of whatever you're working on

04:02.000 --> 04:05.000
at your job right now or whatever you're interested in,

04:05.000 --> 04:09.000
you should get back a list of related IRE resources.

04:09.000 --> 04:15.000
Each one, there's about 33,000 that are currently in the database.

04:15.000 --> 04:21.000
And each one of them has its own sort of detail page or URL

04:21.000 --> 04:24.000
where we present everything we have for that resource, which, you know,

04:24.000 --> 04:28.000
has its name, who came up with it, what type of thing it is.

04:28.000 --> 04:33.000
And then in most cases, we have the associated thing itself,

04:33.000 --> 04:38.000
be it a PDF of the tip sheet that was scanned in or emailed over

04:38.000 --> 04:43.000
or an audio recording of a panel or other event.

04:43.000 --> 04:45.000
Those are the two most common things.

04:45.000 --> 04:48.000
And so 33,000 are in there.

04:48.000 --> 04:52.000
What you'll find if you go in there, if you fish through it,

04:52.000 --> 04:56.000
is that the majority of them are IRE contest entries.

04:56.000 --> 05:00.000
So, you know, through the years when people have applied to win an IRE award,

05:00.000 --> 05:03.000
they've had to fill out a form that explains, you know,

05:03.000 --> 05:08.000
why they should win and talks about the method behind their work.

05:08.000 --> 05:12.000
And that actually accounts for the majority of the 33,000

05:12.000 --> 05:16.000
are those contest entries, which stretch back decades.

05:16.000 --> 05:20.000
In preparing this site, I heard from some of our members

05:20.000 --> 05:23.000
who are passionate users of the contest entries.

05:23.000 --> 05:26.000
I can share with you a couple use cases I heard about

05:26.000 --> 05:29.000
from real world investigative people.

05:29.000 --> 05:33.000
One is an editor here at Reuters who told me that he uses the database

05:33.000 --> 05:37.000
to look for and sniff around topics that he's interested in exploring

05:37.000 --> 05:39.000
and maybe investigating.

05:39.000 --> 05:42.000
And what he'll do is look for maybe classic examples

05:42.000 --> 05:46.000
that he hadn't heard of from decades past.

05:46.000 --> 05:48.000
Maybe they're localized in different places

05:48.000 --> 05:50.000
and ask himself, could I nationalize the story

05:50.000 --> 05:53.000
or is there some lesson I can learn from what's been done in the past?

05:53.000 --> 05:57.000
It also allows him to see whether other people have already covered the waterfront

05:57.000 --> 06:00.000
or whether there might be new stuff to look up.

06:00.000 --> 06:05.000
His view is that newspaper archives are so decayed

06:05.000 --> 06:08.000
and the internet is so biased towards the present

06:08.000 --> 06:12.000
that he often can't find the same information when he searches in Google

06:12.000 --> 06:16.000
and that looking at the contest entries gives him a more direct look

06:17.000 --> 06:20.000
at what investigative reporters and editors have been doing.

06:20.000 --> 06:22.000
So that's one case.

06:22.000 --> 06:25.000
Another case I heard was from people applying for awards.

06:25.000 --> 06:29.000
So you could look at the applications from the previous year

06:29.000 --> 06:31.000
and say, hey, who won and why did they apply

06:31.000 --> 06:33.000
and what things did they emphasize or not?

06:33.000 --> 06:36.000
And that can help you write a better package yourself.

06:36.000 --> 06:38.000
That was another tip I heard.

06:38.000 --> 06:41.000
And then a third use case, which I did not anticipate

06:41.000 --> 06:43.000
but makes a lot of sense,

06:43.000 --> 06:48.000
is I think a number of journalism educators use the contest entries

06:48.000 --> 06:52.000
to help their students learn about the methods and practices

06:52.000 --> 06:55.000
of kind of like the great stories in IRE history.

06:55.000 --> 06:58.000
So if you're having your students study the Boston Globe

06:58.000 --> 07:01.000
child sexual abuse story

07:01.000 --> 07:04.000
or pick your greatest hit of investigative journalism,

07:04.000 --> 07:10.000
if you find the contest entries for those stories,

07:10.000 --> 07:14.000
you can often get a lot of insider detail about how the data analysis

07:14.000 --> 07:18.000
was done or the story was put together, the kind of behind-the-scenes stuff

07:18.000 --> 07:21.000
that could be really helpful for students to learn.

07:21.000 --> 07:25.000
So that was the third interesting use case I heard about contest entries.

07:25.000 --> 07:30.000
The number two most common thing in the database are, of course, the tip sheets,

07:30.000 --> 07:32.000
which I suspect are the most popular.

07:32.000 --> 07:36.000
I don't have data to back that up, but that's going to be all the materials

07:36.000 --> 07:40.000
presentations through the years with the PDFs attached.

07:40.000 --> 07:44.000
We have 6,000 of those assembled there in this data set.

07:44.000 --> 07:48.000
There are a few duplicates and kind of reruns from year to year,

07:48.000 --> 07:51.000
but it's pretty fresh, pretty fresh and pretty unique.

07:51.000 --> 07:54.000
And then the third large group are the audio recordings.

07:54.000 --> 08:00.000
These are all the panel discussions and events that happen at IRE conferences.

08:00.000 --> 08:04.000
A large share of those are put down to tape,

08:04.000 --> 08:08.000
and you can listen to MP3s of Bob Woodward and Leonard Downey

08:08.000 --> 08:10.000
talking about this or that,

08:10.000 --> 08:15.000
or Sarah Cohen schooling you up on the database frame of mind way back when.

08:15.000 --> 08:20.000
You also are able to listen to the audio recordings of more recent events as well

08:20.000 --> 08:25.000
and find stuff related to topics you're interested in.

08:25.000 --> 08:29.000
And for me, as someone who was really exploring this archive in depth

08:29.000 --> 08:31.000
for the first time making the site,

08:31.000 --> 08:35.000
I had a lot of fun going back and listening to some of the older stuff

08:35.000 --> 08:40.000
and tuning into the golden oldies.

08:40.000 --> 08:44.000
Every asset in here has a common set of metadata

08:44.000 --> 08:48.000
or columns in the spreadsheet that go with each record,

08:48.000 --> 08:50.000
and some are filled in better than others.

08:50.000 --> 08:52.000
This is not a perfect data set.

08:52.000 --> 08:54.000
This release we're doing is really a first effort

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
just to get this stuff up and out,

08:56.000 --> 08:58.000
and there's a lot of room for improvement.

08:58.000 --> 09:00.000
And this is one of those areas where we think we can improve.

09:00.000 --> 09:04.000
You can see why everything has a category and a title and description.

09:04.000 --> 09:08.000
Our author's field isn't very well structured or formatted.

09:08.000 --> 09:10.000
It's a little loosey goosey.

09:10.000 --> 09:15.000
And we've got some pretty big gaps as far as the year or date

09:15.000 --> 09:17.000
when the records were created,

09:17.000 --> 09:19.000
which means that when you do a search and try to sort

09:19.000 --> 09:21.000
for what the most recent document is,

09:21.000 --> 09:24.000
which is a really common use case we heard from people,

09:24.000 --> 09:26.000
we don't have a perfect solution yet.

09:26.000 --> 09:28.000
And that's something we want to get better at

09:28.000 --> 09:31.000
bringing up these numbers that you see here on this chart

09:31.000 --> 09:32.000
is one of our goals,

09:32.000 --> 09:36.000
and we want to gradually add more and more extra stuff on as well.

09:36.000 --> 09:41.000
I will jump in here to say that this is much more

09:41.000 --> 09:46.000
of a problem of a past resource problem.

09:46.000 --> 09:53.000
The efforts of the staff have really helped us have more data,

09:53.000 --> 09:56.000
more metadata information about more recent resources,

09:56.000 --> 10:00.000
tip sheets, conference entries, things like that.

10:00.000 --> 10:06.000
But there are quite a few resources from older conferences,

10:06.000 --> 10:12.000
events where that information isn't present.

10:12.000 --> 10:16.000
And oftentimes that affiliation, which is one of the lowest ones,

10:16.000 --> 10:20.000
essentially that's being extracted from people's names

10:20.000 --> 10:23.000
and information on, say, a tip sheet.

10:23.000 --> 10:27.000
And in those cases, if there wasn't really a standard,

10:27.000 --> 10:30.000
people might just put their names and emails on a tip sheet.

10:30.000 --> 10:35.000
And so there's literal cases where the information isn't there

10:35.000 --> 10:37.000
and we have to either sort of derive it

10:37.000 --> 10:38.000
or maybe we won't get it,

10:38.000 --> 10:42.000
or we have to wait until someone might claim it.

10:42.000 --> 10:45.000
Yep.

10:45.000 --> 10:51.000
And this database that you can search with uses

10:51.000 --> 10:53.000
what's called semantic search.

10:53.000 --> 10:56.000
And so this is kind of an AI-flavored version of search

10:56.000 --> 11:00.000
that goes a little beyond just doing kind of strict keyword matching

11:00.000 --> 11:02.000
of what you're putting in.

11:02.000 --> 11:04.000
Kind of under the hood, inside the database,

11:04.000 --> 11:09.000
we've had a machine learning system kind of break up each asset

11:09.000 --> 11:12.000
into kind of its component parts

11:12.000 --> 11:15.000
using the kind of fancy vectorization and AI stuff

11:15.000 --> 11:17.000
we hear so much about these days.

11:17.000 --> 11:21.000
And that allows it to take your query, what you search in there,

11:21.000 --> 11:24.000
and find something closer to what you really mean.

11:24.000 --> 11:27.000
And it also allows us to sort of rate documents

11:27.000 --> 11:29.000
based on their similarity to each other

11:29.000 --> 11:31.000
and other interesting things.

11:31.000 --> 11:34.000
All of the code that does this is open source on GitHub

11:34.000 --> 11:38.000
on the IRE apps profile page, if anyone's interested.

11:38.000 --> 11:40.000
And you can see the Python code we do.

11:40.000 --> 11:44.000
I'd be happy to get nerdy in the question and answer portion

11:44.000 --> 11:45.000
and go into the details of this,

11:45.000 --> 11:47.000
but I don't want to bore you too much.

11:47.000 --> 11:51.000
But we can brag and say we do have an AI-enabled search,

11:51.000 --> 11:54.000
which is pretty cool and was a big learning experience

11:54.000 --> 11:57.000
for me putting this together.

11:57.000 --> 12:00.000
After you do your search, you see a result page kind of like this,

12:00.000 --> 12:03.000
which takes the result of that AI funnel

12:03.000 --> 12:06.000
and says, here's what we think are the best matches for you,

12:06.000 --> 12:10.000
which you then can pick and see from the list how you like.

12:10.000 --> 12:12.000
You're able to tune that search by, say,

12:12.000 --> 12:14.000
limiting it to different categories.

12:14.000 --> 12:17.000
Say you only want to search tip sheets or audio.

12:17.000 --> 12:21.000
You're able to just check and uncheck those boxes and do that.

12:21.000 --> 12:24.000
You're allowed to sort newest and oldest in addition to relevance,

12:24.000 --> 12:28.000
but it is imperfect due to those gaps in the data currently.

12:28.000 --> 12:30.000
And if you really, really hate AI,

12:30.000 --> 12:32.000
you can just turn it off there with that little switch

12:32.000 --> 12:36.000
and do a standard keyword search as well.

12:36.000 --> 12:40.000
At the bottom of each of pages, each assets page,

12:40.000 --> 12:42.000
you can see some recommended similar documents.

12:42.000 --> 12:46.000
This is based on that same AI sugar where we're able to say,

12:46.000 --> 12:51.000
you know, this one is most similar to that one and on and on.

12:51.000 --> 12:55.000
I did have an AI bot make these similar scores for my slide,

12:55.000 --> 12:57.000
and I can see that it has titled one,

12:57.000 --> 13:00.000
a guide to ass kicking in the 90s.

13:00.000 --> 13:02.000
I just noticed that.

13:02.000 --> 13:03.000
I don't think that's a real tip sheet.

13:03.000 --> 13:06.000
Maybe it is a real tip sheet. I don't know.

13:06.000 --> 13:08.000
We'll have to look that one up.

13:08.000 --> 13:11.000
As we said at the start, this is member walled.

13:11.000 --> 13:16.000
So just registering with the IRE website is not enough to access this database.

13:16.000 --> 13:20.000
You have to both be registered and have an active membership,

13:20.000 --> 13:23.000
aka have paid your dues in the last year.

13:23.000 --> 13:26.000
If you don't do that, you can't get it. Sorry.

13:26.000 --> 13:30.000
And this is the kind of software tools we're using.

13:30.000 --> 13:32.000
We're using kind of a vectorized database,

13:32.000 --> 13:34.000
which is a newfangled thing to kind of build.

13:34.000 --> 13:37.000
And this site, I began, I'd be happy to talk about the tech later

13:37.000 --> 13:39.000
if people want to get into it.

13:39.000 --> 13:41.000
But, you know, just to wrap up,

13:41.000 --> 13:43.000
I just want to say this is a work in progress.

13:43.000 --> 13:48.000
It's a very simple search that has a couple interesting features,

13:48.000 --> 13:52.000
but there's so much more we probably can and should be doing with this.

13:52.000 --> 13:56.000
We want to hear your feedback on what's broken or not like it should be.

13:56.000 --> 14:01.000
We want to hear your feature requests on what it should be doing, that it's not.

14:01.000 --> 14:03.000
And if you're really passionate about this,

14:03.000 --> 14:08.000
we would love to welcome you into our volunteer creation process.

14:09.000 --> 14:13.000
We have a sort of list of priorities going forward.

14:13.000 --> 14:18.000
Our top priority is to expand this system to make it possible for the IRE

14:18.000 --> 14:21.000
staff to do rolling incremental updates.

14:21.000 --> 14:25.000
Currently, we're just able to do like big database dumps every so often in

14:25.000 --> 14:29.000
that requires like me or Derek to push a button or Cody.

14:29.000 --> 14:32.000
And we'd rather have like an administrative panel

14:32.000 --> 14:34.000
where the staff can be actively improving it.

14:34.000 --> 14:35.000
That's our top priority.

14:35.000 --> 14:37.000
We're going to do that in the coming months.

14:37.000 --> 14:40.000
But then we also have some gaps in the data,

14:40.000 --> 14:43.000
stuff that was lost in the migration, some of these flawed fields.

14:43.000 --> 14:46.000
We want to try to start cleaning that up.

14:46.000 --> 14:47.000
And then finally,

14:47.000 --> 14:50.000
we have some really interesting stuff associated with the assets.

14:50.000 --> 14:54.000
We haven't integrated yet like the OCRing of all the documents,

14:54.000 --> 14:56.000
the transcribing of all the audio files.

14:56.000 --> 14:59.000
We do have all those associated transcripts,

14:59.000 --> 15:02.000
but we have not yet integrated those into the search or the interface.

15:02.000 --> 15:04.000
You can't see them on the site.

15:04.000 --> 15:06.000
Your search doesn't benefit from them.

15:06.000 --> 15:09.000
And we'd really like to get around to that before too long too.

15:09.000 --> 15:11.000
So that's kind of our roadmap.

15:11.000 --> 15:15.000
The whole thing was done on a volunteer basis by really the three of us

15:15.000 --> 15:20.000
and our colleagues on the volunteer task force.

15:20.000 --> 15:25.000
So be nice to us because this was a nice weekend thing.

15:25.000 --> 15:30.000
But we definitely also want your brutal honesty about how it can

15:30.000 --> 15:32.000
and should be better as well.

15:32.000 --> 15:35.000
And that's really my spiel.

15:35.000 --> 15:38.000
And I don't know if Cody has an agenda beyond this,

15:38.000 --> 15:43.000
but I'd love to hear from folks of what they think about it,

15:43.000 --> 15:50.000
how they use the IRE archives and how we can make them better.

15:50.000 --> 16:01.000
No, let's go ahead and open it up if people want to chat.

16:02.000 --> 16:05.000
Okay, I can get us started.

16:05.000 --> 16:06.000
Hi, everybody.

16:06.000 --> 16:08.000
Thanks so much for sharing.

16:08.000 --> 16:11.000
Well, for building the tool or for sharing it with everyone.

16:11.000 --> 16:16.000
One piece of feedback I gave in the Slack that I wanted to follow up on

16:16.000 --> 16:21.000
was categorization or the possibility of categorization.

16:21.000 --> 16:25.000
I had mentioned that for me as someone who is new to journalism,

16:25.000 --> 16:27.000
I'm not aware of what I don't know.

16:27.000 --> 16:31.000
And so even coming up with what to search for is hard

16:31.000 --> 16:35.000
when I'm presented with just a search bar on the homepage.

16:35.000 --> 16:38.000
So definitely something like that would be really helpful

16:38.000 --> 16:39.000
as a future request for me.

16:39.000 --> 16:42.000
You'd be interested in kind of browsing topics you're interested in.

16:42.000 --> 16:43.000
Am I hearing that right?

16:43.000 --> 16:44.000
Exactly.

16:44.000 --> 16:47.000
Yeah, we've heard that you're not the only person who said that.

16:47.000 --> 16:51.000
You know, we had several other people at NyCar raise the same question.

16:51.000 --> 16:53.000
They're kind of like, I want to nose around.

16:53.000 --> 16:56.000
I want to walk through the stacks a little bit, you know.

16:56.000 --> 17:02.000
And I could imagine, you know, we don't currently have kind of tags

17:02.000 --> 17:05.000
or categories beyond the sort of like, this is a tip sheet.

17:05.000 --> 17:07.000
This is a contest entry level stuff.

17:07.000 --> 17:11.000
And so getting that, like applying those groupings

17:11.000 --> 17:15.000
is like something maybe AI can help us with or I don't know what.

17:15.000 --> 17:16.000
Like, so that's a challenge.

17:16.000 --> 17:18.000
And we were actually talking about that earlier today.

17:18.000 --> 17:20.000
Like, how could we do that in a disciplined way?

17:20.000 --> 17:24.000
A second idea we've heard, I wonder what you would think of this,

